  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The position vector $r(t)$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=\u27e84t-{t}^{3},2{t}^{2}+t\u27e9.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a.$",
  "worked_solution": "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-{t}^{3}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+t)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e84-3{t}^{2},4t+1\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4-3{t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t+1)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-6t,4\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$",
